the  situation in afghanistan has  deteriorated and as i said in the house  of commons a few weeks ago  we could see this coming for for some  time and  we'd have to make preparations to to  pull out and that's what's going on now  that moment has now come so in the  course the next few days we will see  the vast bulk of uk  embassy staff officials come back and  we'll step up our efforts to bring back  those afghans who have helped us help  the uk helped international forces  throughout the uh the last 20 years  and uh in addition to the the the 2000  who have already come out and to help  them we're sending out another  team of home office uh officials to to  help them with their their applications  and and get them out  and uh  obviously we are appealing to to those  who uh have  association with uh the uk who who want  to come out uh now to come forward and  identify themselves i think what i would  say at this at this stage is it is very  difficult obviously uh but i think the  uk can be extremely proud of what has  been done in afghanistan over the last  20 years don't forget that the combat  mission ended in in 2014 there isn't a  military  solution  but thanks to the efforts of of the uk  uh armed services uh all the sacrifices  they made uh we have seen uh no al-qaeda  attacks on against the west uh for a  very long time  and there are millions of girls and  young women who've been educated uh in  afghanistan thanks to the efforts of  of the uk and british armed services i  think people can be can be very proud of  that but things are going in the  opposite direction now how worried are  you that afghanistan is going to become  a breeding ground for terrorists and  isn't there an argument to go in the  opposite direction and to perhaps have a  permanent troop presence once again  i think we've got to be realistic about  the abilities of the  the uk or any power to impose a military  solution a combat  solution in afghanistan  what we certainly can do  is work with all our partners uh  in the region and around the world who  share an interest in uh with us in  preventing afghanistan from once again  becoming a breeding ground for terror i  think anybody uh in government in kabul  is going to recognize that the the west  the uk have a permanent interest in  making sure that that does not happen  and so we are going to use our  diplomatic our our political our  overseas development aid budget uh  leverage uh to make sure uh that we  exert what pressure we can and uh we  will continue to do that but i think the  idea of a of a military or a combat  solution is uh is is not the one that we  should be pursuing right now it's a grim  reality as we approach the 20th  anniversary of 9 11. what do you say to  military families who lost their loved  ones in afghanistan some of whom are now  saying they feel that their loved ones  died in vain  i  first of all i i  share their  uh the sorrow of all the families who've  lost loved ones over the last 20 years  the the 457  british troops who were  serving men and women who were killed in  the in that conflict uh many of them in  in helmand uh i thank them uh for the  immensity of their sacrifice thank their  families uh for what they have they've  gone through and  i know how they must feel at the moment  but i must say that i don't believe it  was  in vain because i do think when you look  back at what has happened over the last  uh 20 years there was a massive effort  to deal with a particular problem uh  that everybody will remember after 9 11  and that was successful  and to a very large extent the threat  from al-qaeda on the streets of  our capital around the uk around the  whole of the west uh was greatly greatly  reduced and there was the further  advantage  that millions i think three million uh  girls young women were educated uh who  would not otherwise have been educated  and so what i would say to to to those  families now is uh i i understand what  you what you feel but i believe that it  was uh it was right it was it was worth  it and what we must do now  is not uh turn our backs  on afghanistan but continue as a member  of the security council uh  someone a country deeply involved in the  strategic future of the area to work  with our partners and make sure  that the government of kabul does not  allow  that country again to be a breeding  ground for terror ben wallace has  suggested it was a mistake for the u.s  to withdraw troops he said the doha deal  was a rotten  deal do you agree with your defense  secretary and if so why weren't we  pushing against it at the time  the  american government obviously has  decided to take a particular course of  action and that is one that uh has the  inevitable logical consequence that i  set out in the house of commons  several weeks ago and the situation has  now evolved in exactly the direction  that we we said it would we have to deal  with those consequences

